 ### [Checkpointing for Reverse-Mode Memory Optimization](8843-checkpointing-for-reverse-mode-memory-optimization.md)
-An optimization to reduce the memory complexity of reverse-mode automatic differentiation by storing only a subset of intermediate variables (checkpoints) and recomputing the loop segments between them during the reverse pass. Introduced by Andreas Griewank in 1992, it achieves memory complexity of $O(\log(N))$ at the cost of increasing time complexity to $O(N \log(N))$, where $N$ is the loop length.
