 ## Background
-The Adjoint Method, also known as Reverse-Mode Automatic Differentiation, was introduced by Andreas Griewank in 1992 [S1]. It is widely used in differentiable rendering for inverse problems such as transfer function reconstruction, volume reconstruction, and scene optimization [S2]. It serves as a baseline in inverse rendering optimization experiments [S6, unofficial]. Implementations often use custom CUDA operations with JIT compilation (e.g., via NVRTC) to integrate with deep learning frameworks like PyTorch [S2].
+The Adjoint Method is a standard technique in automatic differentiation, widely used in differentiable rendering for inverse problems such as transfer function reconstruction, volume reconstruction, and scene optimization [S2]. It serves as a baseline in inverse rendering optimization experiments [S6, unofficial]. Implementations often use custom CUDA operations with JIT compilation (e.g., via NVRTC) to integrate with deep learning frameworks like PyTorch [S2]. Checkpointing for reverse-mode memory optimization was introduced by Andreas Griewank [S1].
 
